  <section id="safety">
    <h2>Essential Safety Guidelines for River Swimming</h2>
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/2901209/pexels-photo-2901209.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=800&h=500&fit=crop" alt="Safe wild swimming location in UK river with clear water" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <p>
      Safety must always be the top priority when wild swimming in rivers in the UK. The British waterways, while beautiful, can be unpredictable and potentially dangerous without proper precautions. <strong>Never swim alone</strong> - always have at least one person on the bank who can call for help if needed. Cold water shock is a serious risk in UK rivers, even during summer months, as water temperatures rarely exceed 20°C (68°F) and can be significantly colder in Scottish rivers and chalk streams. Acclimatize gradually by entering the water slowly and never dive or jump into unfamiliar waters.
    </p>
    <p>
      Understanding river currents is crucial for safe swimming in major rivers in the UK. Currents can be deceptively strong, even in rivers that appear calm on the surface. Always swim parallel to the bank rather than attempting to swim across rivers, and be aware that currents are typically strongest in the center of the river channel. Avoid swimming near weirs, locks, and other man-made structures where dangerous undertows can form. Check local conditions before swimming, and never enter the water after heavy rainfall when rivers are swollen and fast-flowing.
    </p>
    <h3>Key Safety Rules</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Check water quality</strong> using official monitoring data from the Environment Agency or SEPA</li>
      <li><strong>Tell someone</strong> where you're swimming and when you expect to return</li>
      <li><strong>Wear a bright swim cap</strong> to remain visible to others</li>
      <li><strong>Know your limits</strong> and don't swim beyond your comfortable distance</li>
      <li><strong>Avoid alcohol</strong> before or during wild swimming sessions</li>
      <li><strong>Learn to float</strong> as a survival technique if you get into difficulty</li>
      <li><strong>Exit the water</strong> if you feel excessively cold or experience unusual symptoms</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="best-locations">
    <h2>Best Locations for Wild Swimming in UK Rivers</h2>
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/417173/pexels-photo-417173.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=800&h=500&fit=crop" alt="Beautiful UK river swimming spot with natural surroundings" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <p>
      The map of rivers in UK territories reveals numerous excellent locations for wild swimming, each offering unique characteristics and experiences. <strong>The River Wye</strong> in Wales and England is renowned for its beautiful scenery and multiple safe swimming spots, particularly in the upper reaches near Hay-on-Wye. The river's relatively gentle flow in summer months makes it ideal for beginners, while the stunning Wye Valley provides a spectacular backdrop for your swim. <strong>The River Thames</strong> upstream from London offers designated swimming areas, including the historic swimming spots near Oxford where generations have enjoyed the water.
    </p>
    <p>
      Scotland's rivers provide exceptional wild swimming opportunities in pristine environments. <strong>The River Tay</strong>, Britain's largest river by volume, has numerous safe pools and swimming spots away from the main current. The <strong>River Dee</strong> near Ballater offers crystal-clear Highland water in a breathtaking setting, though swimmers should be prepared for cold temperatures even in summer. For those exploring rivers in the UK seeking chalk stream experiences, the <strong>River Itchen</strong> and <strong>River Test</strong> in Hampshire provide uniquely clear waters, though swimming access is limited due to conservation concerns.
    </p>
    <h3>Top Wild Swimming Spots</h3>
